Description: fftools/ffmpeg_filter: continue the output of agapfill when a filter graph is rebuilt
 ffmpeg builds a new filter graph when the parameters of an input change
 mid-stream, for example when an AC-3 stream changes from 5.1 to stereo.
 The agapfill filter of the new graph started from nothing. With its
 start option it wrote the leading silence a second time: a source of
 10.05 s that begins at 2 s came out as 19.07 s. A gap at the change
 stayed a hole, because only the old filter knew where the audio had
 stopped, and an overlap at the change made the output step backward.
 .
 The filter gains two commands. position reports where its output
 stands, and resume makes a filter continue from such a report. Before
 send_frame() replaces a graph, it notes the position of every agapfill
 filter. configure_filtergraph() makes the filter of the same name in
 the new graph resume from it. A filter that took no frame reports
 nothing, so its successor starts afresh and still writes the leading
 silence. That is the case after a seek whose discarded frames hold the
 change.
 .
 Filter names differ when their case does, but the positions were noted
 in a dictionary whose keys matched regardless of case. The position of
 agapfill@A replaced that of agapfill@a, so agapfill@a of the new graph
 started from nothing, and the source above came out as 19.07 s again.
 The positions now match by case.
 .
 resume counts the position in the new graph's sample rate. When the rate
 rises, a position near the end of the 64-bit range can pass it, and
 av_rescale_q_rnd() then answers INT64_MIN, which means no timestamp. A
 position of 9 x 10^18 samples at 44.1 kHz became a gap that never ended
 at 48 kHz. Such a position now stops at the largest or the smallest
 timestamp, as the output position does.
 .
 The filter writes a gap one frame of silence for each frame its output
 asks for, and holds the frame after the gap, and the frames behind it,
 until then. ffmpeg freed a graph with those frames in it when another
 input of the same graph changed, for example the frame size of a video.
 A gap of 30 s under way at such a change lost 4,096 to 16,384 samples
 and the rest of the silence. The pending command reports the frames a
 filter has not output yet, and send_frame() now asks the outputs behind
 each agapfill filter for frames until none is pending. It does not ask an
 output whose consumer has finished, for example after -frames, which
 would only discard the rest of the gap. A graph may hold a cycle, for
 example through the feedback filter, and the search for the outputs
 behind a filter followed it until the stack overflowed. The search now
 enters each filter once.
 .
 A filter behind agapfill can end its output inside a gap, for example
 atrim, while a filter behind that one goes on writing frames of its own,
 for example apad. agapfill kept the frame from after the gap, which could
 no longer leave, and pending reported it. send_frame() then asked for
 frames without end and never built the new graph. agapfill now drops the
 frame it holds once its output has ended, and pending reports none.
 .
 No frame comes when a filter behind agapfill waits for another input of
 the same graph, for example amix, and that input is the one whose change
 starts the rebuild. ffmpeg then freed the graph with the frame from after
 the gap and the frames queued behind it. position reported the end of
 the held frame. When no frame had queued behind it, the new filter joined
 the next frame to the old output: in a gap from 2 s to 32 s rebuilt at
 5 s, the audio from 32.1 s came out at 4.99 s. position now reports the
 end of the last frame the filter has output. The new filter then fills
 the time of the frames that were lost with silence, and the audio after
 them keeps its own time. Until the filter has output an input frame, it
 reports the smallest timestamp, so a lead that start began is filled up
 to the new filter's first frame however short the rest of it is.
 .
 A split behind agapfill can feed an output and such a waiting filter.
 The output kept giving frames, so send_frame() asked for the whole rest
 of the gap, and the waiting filter kept every frame of it until the graph
 was freed: with a gap of 10 minutes, ffmpeg peaked at 232 MB instead of
 14 MB. send_frame() now stops asking as soon as one output behind
 agapfill gives no frame, and the frames agapfill holds become silence as
 above.
 .
 send_frame() asked the filters in the order the graph lists them, which is
 the order the graph description declares them, and asked each once. Asking
 one agapfill filter for the rest of its gap can hand frames to another
 agapfill filter behind it, and the description may declare that one first.
 ffmpeg then freed the graph with the frames the filter behind held, the
 frame from after the gap among them, and the new graph wrote their time as
 silence. send_frame() now walks the filters again until it finds none to
 ask, and does not ask a filter again once its asking has stopped.
 .
 The asking still keeps the other inputs of the graph waiting. With
 -shortest, the audio of an output can then run past its video, to about
 the end of the gap less -shortest_buf_duration. max_gap bounds that.
